"A second definition looks to the maker. In 1897 Leo Tolstoy, by then more preacher than novelist, wrote that art is a human activity, consisting in this, that one man consciously, by means of certain external signs, hands on to others feelings he has lived through, and that other people are infected by these feelings, and also experience them. A boy who meets a wolf and then tells the story so that his listeners feel his fear has made art. By this test Tolstoy rejected most of Shakespeare and Beethoven as insincere or unintelligible to plain people, and praised peasant songs.": "4a7d3ce2678d",
"A third definition looks at the work alone. In 1914 the English critic Clive Bell asked what a Persian bowl, a Chinese carpet, a Giotto fresco, and a Cézanne have in common, and answered: lines and colours combined in a particular way, certain forms and relations of forms, stir our aesthetic emotions. He called this significant form. What a picture shows is irrelevant, and Bell dismissed a famous painting of a crowded railway station, full of lifelike detail, as interesting and not art at all.": "f438487edf3b",
"Answer each question by your own lights.": "f09ea4466951",
"Bells definition goes round in a circle.": "7df62cbb4c53",
"China arrived at this view first and never held the other. The Book of Documents says that poetry speaks what the heart is set on, and the preface to the Book of Songs explains: In the heart it is intent. Sent forth in words it is poetry. Chinese critics took it for granted that a poem or a piece of calligraphy shows the person who made it. The weakness of the expression theory lies elsewhere. A cry of pain expresses a feeling and is not a poem, and much great art was made to order by people whose feelings we know nothing about.": "fbc50b2aad22",
"Chinese critics took it for granted that a poem shows its maker.": "c5206d9a0664",
"Chinese painters had long agreed that likeness is not the point. Around 500 CE Xie He listed six principles of painting and put first spirit resonance, which means vitality. Fidelity to the object came third. Su Shi wrote in the eleventh century: To judge a painting by its likeness is to see as a child sees. What these writers valued, though, was the life in the brushwork and the character of the painter that it revealed, which is nearer to expression than to Bells pure pattern. Bells definition has a further problem. When asked what makes a form significant, he could only say that it is the kind that moves us aesthetically, and the definition goes round in a circle.": "c31a291d38d9",
"Duchamp resigned from the board in protest.": "359fdb9888ef",
"For Su Shi, likeness is beside the point.": "15a15a6895c5",
"In April 1917 the Society of Independent Artists held its first exhibition in New York. Its rule was simple: any artist who paid the fee could show a work, and there would be no jury. One entry was a porcelain urinal, laid on its back, signed R. Mutt 1917, and titled Fountain. The directors argued, and the object was kept out of sight behind a partition for the length of the show. Marcel Duchamp, a French artist who sat on the board, resigned in protest. A small magazine edited by his friends then printed a defense: Whether Mr Mutt with his own hands made the fountain or not has no importance. He CHOSE it. He took an ordinary article, the writer said, and created a new thought for that object.": "16bf2fd1a4b3",
"It was put forward and taken up as art.": "6521f679ba3a",
"John Dewey thought all four definitions looked in the wrong place. They ask what kind of object a work of art is. In Art as Experience (1934) he argued that art is something that happens between a person and the world, and that the museum piece is only its most concentrated form. A mechanic absorbed in a job well done and a cook who cares about the meal have experiences of the same kind as the painters. A society that locks art in special buildings, he thought, has usually let it drain out of ordinary work.": "61d81823825b",
"Much great art was made to order.": "c75f00f46b7e",
"None of the three could explain Fountain. It imitates nothing, expresses no feeling that anyone can name, and has the same form as every other urinal from the same factory. In 1964 the philosopher Arthur Danto saw Andy Warhols Brillo Boxes, plywood copies of supermarket cartons, and concluded that what makes something art cannot be anything the eye can see. It is an atmosphere of artistic theory, a knowledge of the history of art: an artworld. George Dickie turned this into a definition: a work of art is an artifact that someone acting on behalf of the artworld has offered as a candidate for appreciation. On this account Fountain is art for the plain reason that it was put forward and taken up as art.": "5383b922edec",
"That standard cuts both ways, and the tradition drew the other conclusion as well. The Buddha taught in verse and in images that people remembered for a lifetime. Tālapuṭa became a monk, and a long poem under his name is preserved in the canon. Later Buddhists filled Asia with sculpture, painting, chant, and calligraphy, and gave an account of what these are for: they are a finger pointing at the moon, to be valued for what they direct the mind toward and not held on to for themselves. In Chan and Zen, brush painting and calligraphy became practices in their own right, in which the quality of the line was taken to show the state of the mind that made it, which is close to what Xie He meant by spirit resonance (Dharma and Western Philosophy D18).": "6c811e2afc77",
"The artist hands on to others feelings he has lived through.": "f1734f4e566a",
"The early texts are wary of the arts as entertainment. Those who keep the eight precepts on observance days give up dancing, singing, music, and shows, and so do monks and nuns at all times. One discourse explains why. Tālapuṭa, the head of a company of performers, asks the Buddha whether it is true, as his teachers said, that a dancer who makes people laugh is reborn among the laughing gods. Twice the Buddha answers, Enough, chief, let it be. Dont ask me that. Asked a third time, he says that people are already bound by greed, hate, and delusion, and that a performer who presents them with even more arousing things makes them more heedless. Tālapuṭa weeps, and says that he is not weeping at the answer. He weeps because he has been deceived for so long. The test applied is Tolstoys, with a different standard: art is judged by the states of mind it passes on.": "d4cf09160316",
"The institutional theory fits every case, and critics say that this is its fault. It tells us that something is art when the right people call it so, and is silent about why they should. Morris Weitz had argued in 1956 that the search was a mistake from the start. Art, like a game, has no single feature shared by all its instances. They are linked by overlapping resemblances, and each new movement extends the family. On this view the question What is art? has no final answer, and the changes in the answer from one age to the next are a record of what each age cared about.": "5ca16cb0ebb8",
"The object was kept out of sight for the length of the show.": "445e50d88113",
"The oldest definition says that art is imitation. Plato states it in order to attack it. There is the idea of a bed, he says, and there is the bed that a carpenter makes by looking to the idea, and then there is the bed in a painting, which copies only how the carpenters bed looks from one side. The imitator, he concludes, is thrice removed from the king and from the truth. Anyone can do what he does by turning a mirror round and round. Aristotle kept the definition and dropped the contempt: people learn by imitating and take pleasure in recognizing a likeness. For two thousand years most European theory began here. The trouble is that music imitates nothing in particular, and neither does a temple, a carpet, or a line of calligraphy.": "99fb2678ff45",
"The original was lost soon afterward. The examples now in museums are replicas made decades later. In 2004 five hundred British art professionals voted Fountain the most influential work of the twentieth century. Whatever one thinks of it, it did what a good philosophical example does. It was a case that every existing definition of art got wrong, or else got right for an embarrassing reason.": "105c8df662b0",
"The question has a new form today. A computer program can now produce an image that imitates well, has striking form, and wins a prize at an art fair, while no one involved has had any feeling to express. Whether it is art depends on which of these theories is right, and the answer will affect who is paid and who is credited.": "88459c02c461",
"Well then, here are three beds: one existing in nature, which is made by God, as I think that we may say—for no one else can be the maker? No. There is another which is the work of the carpenter? Yes. And the work of the painter is a third? Yes. [] And the tragic poet is an imitator, and therefore, like all other imitators, he is thrice removed from the king and from the truth? That appears to be so.": "133087780563",
"What are the six principles? The first is spirit resonance, which means vitality. The second is bone method, which means the use of the brush. The third is fidelity to the object in depicting forms. The fourth is conformity to kind in applying colors. The fifth is planning and placing. The sixth is transmission by copying. To judge a painting by its likeness is to see as a child sees. To insist that a poem be on its stated subject is surely not to know poetry.": "117ec14a6432",
"You can no longer rule anything out on principle.": "352ed3529db1",
